# i18n — Russian + English

User-facing text must ship in **both** languages. **Russian (`ru`) is the default/primary** locale; English (`en`) is secondary.

## Stack

- Library: `next-intl`
- Messages: `apps/web/messages/ru.json`, `apps/web/messages/en.json`
- Routing: `apps/web/i18n/routing.ts` (`defaultLocale: 'ru'`, `localePrefix: 'as-needed'`)
- Use `@/i18n/navigation` (`Link`, `redirect`, `useRouter`, `usePathname`) — not `next/link` / `next/navigation` for app routes

## Rules

- Never hardcode UI strings in components/pages (buttons, labels, titles, errors, empty states, toasts, metadata)
- Add every new key to **both** `ru.json` and `en.json` in the same change
- Keep key structure identical across locales
- Prefer nested namespaces by feature (`HomePage.title`, `Profile.edit`, …)
- Russian copy first when drafting; English is a full translation, not a stub
- Code comments stay English-only (see `english-comments` rule)
- API error codes may be machine keys; user-visible messages still go through i18n on the client (or bilingual API payloads if returned to UI)

## Examples

```tsx
// ❌ BAD
<button>Сохранить</button>

// ✅ GOOD
const t = useTranslations('Profile');
<button>{t('save')}</button>
```

```json
// messages/ru.json
{ "Profile": { "save": "Сохранить" } }

// messages/en.json
{ "Profile": { "save": "Save" } }
```

When reviewing or generating UI, reject PRs/diffs that add RU-only or EN-only user-facing strings.
